---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="Cloudy Pool Water: 5 Causes & Fix Order"
  description="Cloudy pool water has five common causes. Check them in this order before spending money on chemicals or equipment. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>Cloudy pool water: the 5 causes and the order to check them</h1>

  <p>
    Cloudy pool water almost always traces back to one of five things: a filter that isn't
    keeping up, an imbalance in basic chemistry, early-stage algae, mineral content from hard
    water, or fine debris stirred up after wind or rain. Checking them in that order — cheapest
    and easiest first — usually finds the cause without wasting money guessing.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool water cloudy or hazy" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>Check in this order</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Filter condition and run time.</strong> A dirty cartridge, a multiport valve stuck
      between settings, or a pump not running long enough each day are the most common and
      cheapest-to-fix causes. High filter pressure compared to a clean baseline is the clearest
      sign. See our guide on <a href="/guides/how-long-should-pool-pump-run-texas/">how long a
      pump should run in Central Texas</a> if run time is in question.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Basic chemistry imbalance.</strong> pH or alkalinity outside the normal range can
      cause haziness on its own, even with clean equipment and no algae present. This is a quick
      test-and-correct fix once you know the numbers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Early-stage algae.</strong> Before algae is visibly green, it can first show up as
      a general haze or dullness in the water. If chemistry checks out but haze persists, this is
      the next suspect — see our guide on{' '}
      <a href="/guides/pool-green-but-pump-running/">green pools despite a running pump</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>High calcium or mineral content.</strong> Central Texas water is limestone-heavy,
      and calcium buildup in the water column can scatter light and cause a persistent milky
      look that chemistry adjustments alone won't fully clear.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fine debris after weather.</strong> Wind and heavy rain stir up fine particulate
      that a filter can take a day or two to fully clear even when everything else is working
      correctly — sometimes cloudy water just needs more filtration time, not a different fix.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why the order matters</h2>
  <p>
    Most homeowners' instinct is to add more chemical the moment water looks off, but if the
    actual cause is a clogged filter or a run-time problem, extra chemical doesn't fix anything —
    it just gets filtered out (or not) at the same rate as before. Checking equipment and basic
    chemistry first, before assuming algae or minerals, saves money and gets to the right answer
    faster.
  </p>

  <h2>A quick way to narrow it down</h2>
  <p>
    If cloudiness appeared suddenly after a storm, weather-stirred debris is the likely
    explanation and patience plus normal filtration is usually the fix. If it's been building
    gradually over days or weeks with no weather event, chemistry or filtration is the more
    likely cause. If the pool has a history of high stabilizer from trichlor tabs, read our guide
    on <a href="/guides/pool-shock-not-working-cya/">why shock sometimes stops working</a> — CYA
    buildup can present as persistent haze before it ever turns visibly green.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Basic chemistry testing and checking filter pressure are safe to do yourself. Call in a
    technician when cloudiness persists despite balanced chemistry and a clean filter (a sign the
    cause is something less obvious, like a mineral issue or an equipment problem you can't
    diagnose from the pad), or when you want it resolved on a firm timeline rather than trial and
    error. {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Is cloudy water always a sign of a serious problem?</h3>
    <p>
      No — mild, temporary haziness after a storm or heavy pool use is common and usually clears
      with normal filtration. Persistent cloudiness that doesn't improve over a few days is what
      is worth investigating further.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I use a clarifier product to fix cloudy water fast?</h3>
    <p>
      Clarifiers can help clump fine particles so the filter catches them more effectively, but
      they treat a symptom, not the underlying cause — if chemistry or filtration is off, the
      haze tends to return once the clarifier's effect wears off.
    </p>
    <h3>Does hard water cloudiness ever go away on its own?</h3>
    <p>
      Rarely without intervention. Calcium-driven haze usually needs a chemical adjustment
      (a scale/stain treatment) or, in persistent cases, a partial drain and refill — it's
      generally not something that clears with time alone the way weather-stirred debris does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Pool water cloudy or hazy" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
